---
name: cluster
description: Keep, extend, and name the groups of events in a record.
---

Below are the groups this record already has, each with its id, its name, and
the events in it. Below those are the groups the record's own rules have just
proposed, and below those the events that are in no group. Return the record's
groups as they should now stand. Each proposed group is where the record's own
rules found a run of events close in time, with the silence before it stated. A
proposed group comes back as one group unless you can say, in one plain
sentence, which recorded shift ties two of them into one story with nothing
settled between. One family has several groups in its history; separate is the
ordinary case.

**Keep what is there.** A group that already exists keeps its id, its name, and
its events. Someone has read that name before. Change one only when you can say
in one plain sentence what in the record makes the old shape wrong. A
better-sounding name is not a reason, and a neater grouping is not a reason. A
group that already exists is not protected when it covers most of the record's
years or when the record's own rules now propose two or more groups inside it
with a settling between them; then say in one sentence what the record shows
and return the groups as they should stand.

**Put each new event where it belongs.** An event in no group joins a group only
when it is close in time to the events already in it and about the same people,
or when a recorded shift shows it was a reaction to what opened that group. The
same people alone is never a reason: one family's people are in most of its
groups. An event years before a group's first event or years after its last
joins only when the record holds shifts between it and the group with nothing
settled in between. Otherwise leave it out.

**When a group opens and when it ends.** A group opens where something disturbs
the family's balance — someone added, someone lost, someone's position changed —
or where a run of events converges. It ends where the record shows the family
settled: a symptom lifting, functioning coming back, a settlement, or a span
with no recorded shift long enough for this family to have adjusted, judged
against how close together the group's own events fall. An event months or
years after the opening still belongs only while nothing in between shows the
family settled; after a settling, a later run about the same people is a new
group. A group is one disturbance of the family's balance. It is never a stage
of the family's life, such as the years a couple lived alone or the years the
children were at home, never the family's ordinary level, and never the whole
span of a symptom that became chronic; a group that would cover most of the
record's years is the level the groups stand out against, not a group.

Every group carries a `name` in plain words this person would recognise and a
`reason`: one sentence saying what the record shows its events have in common.
The name says what opened the group or where the trouble moved, and its time,
in this family's own words; never a name for one part of a group that spans
more. Never a claim the events do not carry.

**Say every change you make, in story.** For each group you did not hand back
exactly as it was given, return `change`: one sentence about what happened, the
way you would say it aloud about this person's history. Not "merged two groups".
Not "added event 41". A change without that sentence is thrown out and you are
asked again.

Never return two groups whose years overlap, and never a group whose years
overlap one listed under GROUPS THIS PERSON MADE: those are fixed, and you
cannot change them. The timeline is one line, and a group's years run from its
first event to its last.

Never write an id that is not listed below, never put one event in two groups,
and never leave out an event listed under `nodalOrShiftIds`.

Every group you return holds at least three events. One or two events on their
own are never a group, so never break a group into pieces smaller than that.

Return one entry per final group: `id` when it is an existing group, `eventIds`,
`name`, `reason`, and `change` when it is not exactly as given.

